==Russian==

===Etymology===
First recorded in 1820 by A. A. Uspensky in the Fenya argot of Vladimir with the meaning 'noble' (provided {{m|ru|клювый}} in the "Suzdal dialect" by Pallas in 1787 is a misprint or typo instead of *кліовый). From the second half of the 19th century, it also appears in thieves' argot.

According to Preobrazhensky's suggestion, supported by Vasmer, it comes from the verb {{m|ru|клевать}} (further from {{inh|ru|sla-pro|*kľьvati}}, from which originated, among others: {{cog|cu|кльвати}}, perfective {{m|cu|клюнѫти}}, {{cog|uk|клювати}}, {{cog|be|клюваць}}, {{cog|bg|кълва́|t=I peck}}, {{cog|sh|кљу̏jем}}, {{m|sh|кљу̀вати}}, {{cog|sl|kljuváti}}, {{m|sl|kljújem}}, Old Czech {{m|zlw-ocs|kl᾽uju|kl᾽uju}}, {{m|zlw-ocs|klvati|klvati}}, {{cog|cs|klít}} (from {{m|cs|*kljuti}}), {{cog|pl|kluć}}, {{m|zlw-opl|klwać}}, {{cog|hsb|kluwać sо|t=tease one another}}, {{cog|dsb|kluju|t=I peel}}, modern {{m|dsb|kluwaś}}). In this case, it may come from fishermen's jargon, with the original meaning '(good) pecking'.

According to an alternative hypothesis by Studinsky, supported by Bondaletov, from Greek kλέος 'glory, honor' (a noticeable number of Greek borrowings of not entirely clear origin are known in the Fenya argot). According to Priyomysheva, difficulties with the entry of the word into the literary language may testify in favour/favor of this version.

Cf. Dalya: {{m|ru|клюжий}} (from {{m|ru|клюдь}}), {{m|ru|клювый}} 'good, good-looking, handsome, stately, prominent'.

